## Skills

| Skill | Path | Purpose |
|-------|------|---------|
{{member-table}}

Member skills are always external repositories, arriving as git submodules cloned from
their own repositories - never as sources authored inside the collection.

New skills are added under `skills/<name>/` and registered here and in `README.md` in
the same change set.

## Layout

| Path                         | Role                                         |
|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|
| `AGENTS.md`                  | Agent entry point for maintaining the repo   |
| `README.md`                  | Human-facing overview and directory tree     |
| `process/member-contract.md` | Shared member-skill behavior contract        |
| `process/session-review.md`  | Collection-level session-review procedure    |
| `process/synchronization.md` | Member and collection fetch/merge/push       |
| `skills/<name>/`             | One directory per member skill package       |
| `work/`                      | Gitignored local workspace, dated daily dirs |

## Usage

- Member submodules must be fetched before any use. Whenever a skill is going to be
  used - a bare collection activation or a request naming a member - first run
  `git submodule update --init` in the collection root. A skill whose files are not
  on disk cannot be loaded, routed to, or reported on.
- Read the member skill's own `SKILL.md` before acting - it is the runtime contract
  for that skill.
- A request naming a member skill loads that skill directly, fetching its submodule
  first if needed. This index only disambiguates requests that name no member.
- A task that names no member is matched against every member's description, not
  just the closest one - all members whose scope covers part of the task are
  fetched and loaded together, and each applies its own procedures to the part it
  covers. Picking a single recommended member and stopping there is wrong when
  several members are applicable.
- A bare `use {{collection-name}}` request enables every member in roster order:
  fetch any unfetched members first, load each fetched member's `SKILL.md` and run
  its own bare-activation procedure, then answer once with the Enablement Response
  below and wait for the named task - the index performs no operation itself.
- A request to merge remote, synchronize, or update the collection and its members
  runs `process/synchronization.md` - fetch every remote first, fast-forward the
  collection then each member, reconcile pins, and push members before the
  collection.
- Maintenance work on the collection itself starts from `AGENTS.md`, not from this
  file.

## Improvement

- "improve {{collection-name}}", "improve the collection", or "review the session for
  lessons" aimed at the collection runs `process/session-review.md`: lessons are
  partitioned by owner, member lessons are delegated to each member's own
  session-review procedure in roster order, and index lessons pass an approval gate
  before touching root files.
- "improve <member>" skips the index and runs that member's own procedure directly.
- `autonomous`, `fully autonomous`, and `verbose` flags on the request apply to the
  root gates and are passed down to every delegated member review - they grant no
  member extra permissions.

## Enablement Response

A bare activation answers the human with one message: a status line naming the
collection and its `metadata.version`, a roster line per member with the member's
own version and purpose (or a "not initialized - run `git submodule update --init`"
hint for an unfetched member), and a prompt for the task.

The member skills' own enablement responses are folded into the roster lines rather
than printed one after another.

The response is informational only: after printing it, wait for the task.
